Description: BROWSE encoder becomes the waveform zoom on the Overview tab
 The BROWSE rotary was bound straight to [Library],MoveVertical, so it only
 ever scrolled the list -- there was no hardware way to zoom the waveforms at
 all, despite the stock mapping's own header advertising "Waveform zoom
 (shift)" for a binding that does not exist anywhere in the XML.
 .
 The encoder now follows the screen instead of picking one job for good:
 with the Overview tab up it zooms, and in Browse/Search it scrolls exactly as
 before. That keeps the only hardware route through a track list -- losing it
 on an appliance whose alternative is a touchscreen would have been a plain
 regression -- while putting zoom on the knob at the moment the waveforms are
 the only thing on screen.
 .
 One detent is one zoom step, applied to both decks from a single target
 rather than stepping each from its own value: the skin stacks both waveforms
 permanently and they are only readable against each other at a common scale,
 so this also pulls them back together if they ever diverge. Clockwise zooms
 in, which means *subtracting*, because waveform_zoom counts how much track is
 on screen. The range is clamped to 1..10 in the script because
 BaseTrackPlayerImpl::slotWaveformZoomValueChangeRequest ignores a request
 outside it rather than clamping into it, so an unclamped target at either end
 would silently do nothing.
 .
 The binding changes from <SelectKnob/> to <Script-Binding/>, which means the
 script receives the raw MIDI byte: MidiController::receive() returns early on
 the script path, before the SelectKnob decode runs. browseTurn() therefore
 does that decode itself (7-bit two's complement, 0x01 = one detent clockwise,
 0x7F = one counter-clockwise) so scrolling behaves exactly as it did.
 .
 LibraryControl now creates [Tab],overview. The control is normally created by
 LegacySkinParser when it parses <SingletonContainer trigger="[Tab],overview">,
 and the parser reuses one that already exists, so this changes nothing about
 how the skin drives it -- but the script reads the key on every detent, and
 engine.getValue() on a control that does not exist logs a warning per call and
 *throws* in developer mode (ControllerScriptEngineBase::logOrThrowError). The
 key therefore has to exist even under a stock skin that never defines it;
 there it simply stays 0 and the encoder keeps its stock scrolling behaviour.
 Creating it in LibraryControl also gets the ordering right: CoreServices (and
 with it Library/LibraryControl) is constructed before the skin is parsed,
 which is in turn before ControllerManager::setUpDevices() starts any script.
 .
 Not changed: the encoder still zooms while the settings overlay is open,
 because that overlay deliberately blocks touch input only and leaves the
 hardware controls live, exactly like the jog and transport buttons.
